# Fixed 2026-11-24, defect 2753, lane-panel258-r1 (panel 258's R1,
# provisional, author ratification pending; the sitting's `handlejump`): a
# callback opens a C handle marked `acquires slot_close`, calls C that
# `longjmp`s over it, and would then have closed it. The program's source
# writes the release; the jump skipped it. Spec section 13: a handle nobody
# consumes aborts when `main` returns, saying how many, and so this program
# still does.
# Symptom: the abort said `this program is missing that many`, accusing the
# program of a release it writes.
# Cause: the handle gate read the count of handles alone, and not the count
# of callbacks entered and never left, which defect 2704 made the heap-block
# gate read.
# Fix: the handle, lease and scratch gates keep their aborts and, where a
# callback never returned, say so: how many, and that a release those frames
# would have run never ran (`runtime/parts/alloc.c`).
extern "fixedbugs-2753-handlejump.h"
    record Slot tag Slot
    function slot_open(n: i64) -> Slot acquires slot_close
    function slot_close(s: Slot consumes)
    function slot_value(s: Slot) -> i64
    function hj_try(cb: (function(i64) -> i64)) -> i64
    function hj_throw()

function body(n: i64) -> i64
    a = slot_open(n: n)
    v = slot_value(s: a)
    hj_throw()
    slot_close(a)
    return v

function main()
    print("result ", hj_try(cb: body))
